On PostgreSQL 18 our orders table has 80 million rows and takes writes all day. I need to ship this migration during business hours:

BEGIN;
ALTER TABLE orders ADD COLUMN public_id uuid NOT NULL DEFAULT gen_random_uuid();
ALTER TABLE orders ADD COLUMN customer_id bigint REFERENCES customers(id);
ALTER TABLE orders ALTER COLUMN customer_id SET NOT NULL;
CREATE INDEX orders_created_at_idx ON orders (created_at);
COMMIT;

Will this block production, and how should I rewrite it?

Our Node.js workers pull jobs from a Postgres table. Each worker runs:

BEGIN ISOLATION LEVEL SERIALIZABLE;
SELECT id FROM jobs WHERE status = 'pending' ORDER BY id LIMIT 1 FOR UPDATE;
UPDATE jobs SET status = 'running' WHERE id = $1;
COMMIT;

With 20 workers they mostly wait on each other, and sometimes we get errors like 'could not serialize access due to concurrent update' that we log and drop. Some sessions also stay 'idle in transaction' for hours after a crash. How should this be done?
